After /cd, the note saying the new directory's .yoyo.toml is not applied is now printed as a yellow ⚠ warning: instead of dim text. The config itself is still not applied, so the user is no safer in the new directory, and #869 stays open. The commit message says both.

What changed (all in src/cd_config_note.rs):
• **How loud it is:** a note that names `[permissions]`, `[directories]`, hooks or MCP servers is a warning. It is printed as `⚠ warning: …` in yellow, or `warning: …` with no symbol or colour codes under plain output. The list of those sections is built from the file's own four label constants rather than typed out again.
• **The one note that stays dim:** "the file exists but could not be read" names no section, and we can't tell what the file sets. Its output is byte-for-byte the same as before.
• **Quiet mode:** neither version of the note is hidden by `--quiet`, and a comment at the print site says why: quiet turns on automatically for piped runs, which nobody watches live (the same reason as the max_tokens warning, #964).

Two premises in the task didn't hold:
• **No "non-safety" note exists.** A config that only sets `model` or `provider` already prints nothing, and all four sections the file detects count as safety sections. So every note about sections the file sets is now a warning, and the near-miss test uses the "could not be read" note instead.
• **There was no quiet gating to keep.** The Day-213 note was never hidden under `--quiet`, and ARCHITECTURE.md already said so. Adding gating to the dim note would have changed its behaviour, so I didn't.

Tests: 6 new, 16 in the module.
• The fixtures go through the real section detector, and one test checks that what the detector finds across them equals the full safety list.
• The warning's exact output is checked with and without plain output (in plain, no escape bytes and no symbol). The dim note is checked with full-string equality in both modes.
• The quiet decision is covered only by a deliberately weak test: it proves is_quiet doesn't appear in the print function, not that the line reaches a terminal.

Positive control: I forced the severity check to always return "dim" in one command, marked NEUTERED. Exactly the three warning tests failed by name and the near-miss tests stayed green. After restoring it, all 16 passed and no NEUTERED marker is left in the file.

Checks: cargo fmt --check, cargo clippy --all-targets -D warnings and the full cargo test all passed with 0 failures. The file is 390 lines, well under the size limit.

Docs: I added an ARCHITECTURE.md bullet under the Day-213 entry and an Unreleased line in the CHANGELOG. dispatch.rs is untouched.

